WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Speaker 1: All right. Let's start today with sort&nbsp;
of an audience question. I think it's a good one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:12.000
This is exactly the type of question I love to&nbsp;
answer. A viewer who I guess goes by the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:19.200
Smash wrote to me about the 2020 election and&nbsp;
said, Dear David, you believe the 2020 election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:25.860
was not stolen and there is no evidence to the&nbsp;
contrary? What would you accept as evidence?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:31.740
What would it take to convince you it was stolen?&nbsp;
Hope you can answer these questions. Thanks. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:37.620
is a fair question because I would be a hypocrite&nbsp;
and I don't want to be a hypocrite in front of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:46.680
of you fine folks. If I demanded that sort of&nbsp;
consideration from others, what evidence would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:52.080
change your mind and then refuse to answer or&nbsp;
explain it myself? And remember very often if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.520
the answer is there is no evidence, that would&nbsp;
change my mind. You're not really thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:02.460
so what evidence would change my mind? The&nbsp;
first thing, whenever you have a conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.140
like this and maybe this will be useful to&nbsp;
some to some people. When we say evidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:14.280
what are we even talking about? And so to be&nbsp;
very clear. For me to be convinced that there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:23.580
an orchestrated voter fraud or election fraud&nbsp;
wherein Donald Trump was the real winner, but Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:30.420
Biden stole it. I would need evidence of some kind&nbsp;
of systematic voter fraud. So the fact that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:36.840
found a bunch of Republicans, you know, a dozen or&nbsp;
so who either voted twice, there's the guy who's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:43.800
junior and he voted for himself and for his dad,&nbsp;
senior, who is no longer alive. Or we find, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:50.820
know, a Democrat that voted illegally via absentee&nbsp;
ballot and then died by Election Day. Like none&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.620
of those things are proof of systematic fraud.&nbsp;
And not only do they not rise mathematically to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.620 --> 00:02:01.260
the theft of an election from Donald Trump,&nbsp;
it's also just like a random person. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:07.200
so that's the first important thing we are looking&nbsp;
for evidence of systematic voter fraud here. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.040
would even go further and say systematic voter&nbsp;
fraud that would have materially changed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.660
results in at least one state, not even the&nbsp;
entire thing, but at least one state. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:21.660
there are two types of claims that the proponents&nbsp;
of these conspiracy theories often make.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:28.980
The first type of claim is that sometimes the&nbsp;
big lie people will make a claim that something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:36.000
happened and that thing did happen. But it just&nbsp;
isn't actually voter fraud even at the individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.400
level, never mind some kind of systematic voter&nbsp;
or election fraud. So I'll give you an example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.560
They say hundreds of thousands of people were&nbsp;
registered in two places or usually it's like at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.540
the state level, they'll say tens of thousands of&nbsp;
people were registered at two different addresses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:57.660
That's not evidence of any kind of fraud. It's not&nbsp;
a crime. It's really common that when people move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.280
they remain registered at the old place. If&nbsp;
they showed up and try to vote at the old place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:07.680
they would be allowed to. But they also might&nbsp;
be registered at the new place. As long as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.440
don't vote twice, there's no issue and there's no&nbsp;
crime. So all of the allegations from the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:21.000
about things that are true but aren't actually a&nbsp;
problem, those I don't consider evidence of voter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.900
fraud because they're quite literally not fraud.&nbsp;
Another example is they say state legislatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:34.200
changed voting rules without altering the&nbsp;
state constitution. Well, that's true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.720
but that's legal. You might not like it. You&nbsp;
might disagree. But under the public health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:46.020
emergency of COVID in 2020, there were state&nbsp;
legislatures that voted without altering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:52.920
state constitution to expand absentee ballot or&nbsp;
whatever. It's not a crime. It's not illegal, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:58.260
it's not evidence of voter fraud. So that's one&nbsp;
bucket things that happened but aren't actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:04.020
evidence of voter fraud. Those don't change my&nbsp;
mind. All right. Then we get to the claims of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:09.420
anomalies. Anomalies? Anomalies. Your&nbsp;
preference. Okay. Like, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:14.820
hundreds of thousands of people didn't really&nbsp;
vote, but there they show up as having voted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:22.800
There has been no proof of those claims. There's&nbsp;
just no proof of that. So if you can bring to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:32.820
proof of some kind of a systematic requesting&nbsp;
and submitting of absentee ballots by someone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:37.200
and you've got to tell me who the someone&nbsp;
is without the knowledge of the actual voter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:44.280
in more than this minute number of cases where&nbsp;
it seems to be a paperwork error, then that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:51.060
would consider that substantial. If you were&nbsp;
an and it can't be, we don't know who did it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:58.620
and we don't know who the people are. But here's&nbsp;
three people who say, I got an absentee ballot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:03.660
but I didn't ask for it. Okay. There were&nbsp;
places that just sent them out. That's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:08.820
fraud either. Okay. So if you can come to me&nbsp;
with evidence that, listen, we have found an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:16.560
IP address from which 100,000 absentee ballots,&nbsp;
10,000, 20,000, something were requested and mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:24.720
sent somewhere, intercepted by someone who filled&nbsp;
them all out and sent them all in. Oh, now we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:30.660
talking about something. We do not have that proof&nbsp;
of people voting in multiple locations that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:35.700
would be interested in. Not just you're registered&nbsp;
in two places, not illegal. Trump's Tiffany Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:40.680
that applied to Tiffany Trump it applied to Mark&nbsp;
Meadows. That's not actually against the law. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:46.620
you had actual forensic digital evidence of voting&nbsp;
machines being used in a nefarious way, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:51.780
you've got Mike Pillow talking about the machines&nbsp;
and you've got all these claims about Dominion did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:59.400
this. No proof Smartmatic did that. No proof.&nbsp;
Okay. If you present actual evidence that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:06.660
can subject to a digital forensic specialist who&nbsp;
can say, hey, you know what? Here's proof. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:13.740
is proof of the manipulation. Here's 10,000 people&nbsp;
who tried to vote for Trump and their votes were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:19.560
counted for Biden. That has not been presented.&nbsp;
Okay. Felons voting in large numbers on purpose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:26.220
mobilized by someone, right. Like five felons&nbsp;
voting illegally because they either didn't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:31.620
they weren't allowed to vote or even deliberately.&nbsp;
Right. That's not actually evidence of fraud at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:35.700
any real scale. It wouldn't make a difference in&nbsp;
even a single state. And there's no evidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:42.360
it's coordinated by anybody in the election fraud&nbsp;
sort of mode that these Republicans claim. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:51.360
other thing is you have to explain to me how the&nbsp;
relationship between the population of a state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:58.560
The known number of registered voters, the number&nbsp;
of people that turned out. And very importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:05.220
remember the people that voted presidential&nbsp;
and down ballot. There is complete coherence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:10.920
between the presidential vote counts&nbsp;
and the votes for Senate or House,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:18.420
etc. If there were some coordinated voter fraud&nbsp;
of this kind, you would expect that if they just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:24.420
messed with the presidential voting alone,&nbsp;
there would be this completely out of sync,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:31.260
these out of sync results between presidential&nbsp;
and Senate or House or whatever. The margins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:35.760
are completely reasonable in all of these, and&nbsp;
the Republicans never like to explain, Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:41.940
I won my election legally on the same ballot&nbsp;
in which it was stolen from Trump. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:47.760
okay. So the ballots, only some of the votes&nbsp;
were fraudulent, but the down ballot stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:53.640
it's completely incoherent. One of the important&nbsp;
themes or ideas I think that's worth mentioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:57.600
here is something that Dr. Steven Novella writes&nbsp;
about in his I forget the name of the book. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.520
like skeptic's guide or something like that.&nbsp;
It's called Anomaly Hunting. And very often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:07.140
you will hear actually the perfect example is&nbsp;
the umbrella man from the JFK assassination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:12.360
And Doctor Novella talks about this in&nbsp;
his book After the Assassination of JFK.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:19.320
Pictures turned up with a guy holding an umbrella&nbsp;
on the grassy knoll or, you know, on the side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:25.200
the parade route at which John F Kennedy was&nbsp;
killed. And you had people who started to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:31.860
it was a sunny day. There's no reason someone&nbsp;
would have an umbrella. And so they start to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:38.220
build up a picture that goes something like, what&nbsp;
are the odds that we have a presidential parade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:45.300
and the president is assassinated? And we're told&nbsp;
this story of a lone gunman up in the window. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:54.120
there's a guy with a huge umbrella on a sunny day&nbsp;
right alongside where JFK was killed. He must be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.120 --> 00:09:01.860
involved because that's so anomalous. But as Dr.&nbsp;
Steven Novella explains, the question should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:06.720
a different one. The question should be because&nbsp;
that does make it sound nefarious in some way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:13.620
But what if you reframe the question and you said,&nbsp;
what are the odds that at a presidential parade?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:23.340
With so many attendees, you find someone who&nbsp;
looks out of place for no particularly nefarious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:29.580
reason. When you ask it like that, a guy with an&nbsp;
umbrella doesn't sound so sinister. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:33.600
we later found out that the guy with the umbrella,&nbsp;
the umbrella was a political protest. I believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:39.780
it was a political protest against maybe&nbsp;
Robert Kennedy. I don't remember exactly. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:44.580
you have to also understand that, you know, if&nbsp;
I said to you, look at this thing that happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:50.760
that's a one in an 8 million chance you would&nbsp;
say, oh, my God, that's got to be something is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:56.280
going on there. Something is acting on that. One&nbsp;
in 8 million things are so rare. New York City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:03.720
has 8 million people. That means every single day&nbsp;
there could be a one in 8 million occurrence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:09.060
it would be mathematically expected. And so a&nbsp;
lot of this stuff that these people look for is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:15.960
sifting through data to find something that sounds&nbsp;
